This project is released into the public domain via the Unlicense.

Part of this code was produced with the help of AI tools. Those tools
were trained on human work — code, documentation, drafts, protocols,
discussions on issue trackers — harvested without consent and without
compensation. That extraction cannot be undone, and no licensing
scheme can retroactively make it ethical. The use of AI in software
production is not, and cannot really be, ethical given how the models
were built.

Dedicating this output to the public domain is not absolution. It is
the minimum decency we can offer: refusing to claim ownership of work
derived from uncompensated human labour, and returning to the commons
what the training corpora drew from in the first place. It is also a
refusal to play the game of "AI-produced code, but with all the usual
proprietary clauses on top".

Contributors are asked to make the same dedication on their own
contributions, see the PATENTS file in this repository.

